00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:05.720
to receive those messages and
send out the notifications.

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:09.680
Now, coming to databases,
we have a few choices here.

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:14.060
For relational databases, we
have Cloud SQL and Spanner,

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:15.800
which are both managed.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:20.840
For generic SQL needs,
MySQL, Postgres, and SQL

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:23.540
Server Cloud SQL is perfect.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:27.440
Spanner is best for massive
scale relational databases

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:29.660
that need horizontal scaling.

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:33.140
It also supports strong
consistency and asset

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:34.340
transactions.

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:37.100
I would say, you
use Spanner when

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:39.440
you need the benefits
of both relational

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:42.409
and non-relational databases.

00:08:42.409 --> 00:08:44.179
For non-relational
databases, we've

00:08:44.179 --> 00:08:48.320
got three major options,
Filestore, Bigtable,

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:50.540
and Memory Store.

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:54.770
The Firestore is a serverless
document database that can be

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:58.550
used as DB-as-a-service,
easy to set up,

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:03.660
and provides fast results to
complex queries in real time.

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:06.860
It also supports
offline data and syncs,

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:09.920
which make it a great
choice for mobile use

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:13.760
cases along with
Web, IoT, and gaming.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:16.610
Bigtable, on the other
hand, is a wide column

00:09:16.610 --> 00:09:19.310
no SQL database
that supports heavy

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:22.680
reads and writes with
extremely low latency.

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:25.640
This makes it a perfect
choice for events, time series

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:28.370
data from IoT devices,
click streams,

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:30.890
add events, fraud,
recommendations,

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:34.640
and other personalization
related use cases.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:37.970
Memory Store is a fully
managed in-memory data

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:41.450
store service for Redis and
Memcached at Google Cloud.

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:45.560
It is best for in-memory
and transient data stores,

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:49.160
offers extremely low latency
and high performance.

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:52.670
Memory Store is great for
web and mobile gaming,

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:56.540
leaderboards, social, chats, and
newsfeed type applications that

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:59.640
require in-memory
database caches as well.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.750
Next part of the
puzzle is analytics.

00:10:02.750 --> 00:10:07.010
We usually have real time
and batch data to ingest.

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:10.250
Real time data could be
content from servers,

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:14.720
such as click stream data
or from IoT device sensors.

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:17.300
Batch data could
be from your logs.

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:19.460
Now, these are,
obviously, just examples.

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:23.330
Once the data is ingested,
we process and enrich it

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:27.020
to clean it up and make it
ready for downstream systems,

00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:30.980
then store it in a data
warehouse for further analysis.

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:35.480
A data warehouse is a storage
where you keep processed data.

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:38.210
The data analysts
then analyze the data

00:10:38.210 --> 00:10:41.330
and create dashboards
for broader consumption.

00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:45.020
Data scientists can pick up that
data from the data warehouse

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:47.450
and use it for creating
machine learning

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:49.580
models to generate predictions.

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:51.470
You could also
directly ingest data

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:54.620
from storage systems
for ML models.

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:57.470
We can ingest the batch
data from Cloud storage

00:10:57.470 --> 00:11:01.070
and real time data from
applications using Pub/sub,

00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:05.300
which is a serverless messaging
service that scales to ingest

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:07.950
millions of events per second.

00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:12.440
Now, data flow can then be used
for processing and enriching

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:14.330
the batch and streaming data.

00:11:14.330 --> 00:11:17.570
It's based on open
source Apache Beam.

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:21.590
If you are in the Hadoop
ecosystem, then use Dataproc.

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:24.530
It is managed Hadoop
and Spark platform

00:11:24.530 --> 00:11:27.590
that is set up to
analyze the data faster,

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:29.570
instead of worrying
about managing

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:32.030
and standing up your
own Hadoop cluster.

00:11:32.030 --> 00:11:36.500
It literally spins up a
cluster in 90 seconds.

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:39.020
BigQuery is a serverless
data warehouse

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:43.370
that can also act as long
term storage and a data lake.

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:46.430
We can run SQL queries
to analyze our data

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:49.580
within BigQuery, and it
literally queries over

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:53.000
petabytes of data and
responds in seconds

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:56.490
due to the underlying separation
of storage and compute,

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:58.790
which is unique to BigQuery.

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:00.620
We can use this
data from BigQuery

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:04.370
to create a dashboard in
Looker and Data Studio.

00:12:04.370 --> 00:12:07.490
It also offers machine
learning capabilities,

00:12:07.490 --> 00:12:09.770
so we can create
machine learning models

00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:14.090
and make predictions by just
writing some SQL statements.

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:19.340
Now, for ML and AI projects,
we can use the data in BigQuery

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:23.600
or Cloud storage to train
our models in Vertex AI.

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:27.260
Or we can import our
media files, image,

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:31.430
and other unstructured data
from Cloud storage as well.

00:12:31.430 --> 00:12:33.830
In machine learning
and AI, our options

00:12:33.830 --> 00:12:38.150
depend on whether we want to
create our own custom model

00:12:38.150 --> 00:12:40.910
or use a pre-trained model.

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:45.470
My recommendation is to always
start with a pre-trained model,

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:47.660
see if it works
for your use case.

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:49.910
The most common
use cases are all

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:54.890
covered, including images,
text, video, and tabular data.

00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:58.640
If a pre-trained model does
not work for your use case,

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:02.030
then use the AutoML
model in Vertex AI

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:05.540
to train a custom model
on your own data set.

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:09.140
AutoML supports all
the common use cases.

00:13:09.140 --> 00:13:12.650
You can evaluate the
model and look at metrics

00:13:12.650 --> 00:13:14.480
to improve your model.

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:17.960
It deploys the model
for you and provides you

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:20.750
with an EPI endpoint.

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:23.690
In case you have lots of machine
learning and data science

00:13:23.690 --> 00:13:26.750
expertise in-house,
that is only when

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:29.540
I would say, write
your own custom model

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:31.830
code in the framework
of your choice,

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:34.460
such as TensorFlow,
PyTorch, and then

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:38.540
train your model on a prebuilt
or a custom container.

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:42.710
For operations, monitoring,
debugging, and troubleshooting,

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:45.740
Cloud Operations Suite
offers all the tools

00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:50.060
needed to keep track of the
resources and applications.

00:13:50.060 --> 00:13:52.340
When it comes to
security, understand

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:56.060
that security in Cloud is
a shared responsibility.

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:59.750
Google Cloud offers the
inherent infrastructure security

00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:01.610
and the tools that
you would need

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:05.750
to protect your application,
data, and your software.

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:10.010
The data is already encrypted
at rest and in transit.

00:14:10.010 --> 00:14:15.140
You can also bring your own
custom keys for encryption too.

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:19.670
For data loss prevention and
protecting PII information,

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:25.100
you have DLP, which helps mask
and tokenize sensitive data

00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:30.170
in BigQuery, Cloud Storage,
or even on premises.

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:33.320
For user authentication,
you have Cloud Identity,

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:36.650
which checks if the user
is who they say they are.

00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:40.490
And for authorization,
you have Cloud IAM,

00:14:40.490 --> 00:14:44.270
where you can define
who has access to what.

00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:46.850
Security Command
Center continuously

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:50.420
monitors your Google Cloud
environment for visibility

00:14:50.420 --> 00:14:53.420
into the system,
discover vulnerabilities,

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:56.600
detect threats, and
show that, so you

00:14:56.600 --> 00:14:58.940
can take actions on them.

00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:00.980
There are lots of
other security features

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:02.720
that I did not dive into here.

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:04.670
But go ahead and
play around when

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:06.290
you create your own project.

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:09.800
We just took a tour of most
of the Google Cloud services

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:13.220
for an example
application, foo.com.

00:15:13.220 --> 00:15:19.050
We looked at some services for
networking, compute storage,

00:15:19.050 --> 00:15:24.470
databases, data analytics,
machine learning, security,

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:25.790
and operations.

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:28.770
There is definitely a
lot more to explore.

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:31.160
But I am confident this
will get you started

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:33.350
with the right background.

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:34.735
Now, it's your turn.

00:15:34.735 --> 00:15:37.910
I encourage you to sign
up for a free trial

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:40.250
